// Alexandra: the Victorian lake fly named for Princess Alexandra, a silver body under a peacock sword wing with red slips.

id alexandra
name "Alexandra"
aliases "Lady of the Lake"
type wet
imitates baitfish attractor
naturals fry-minnow
difficulty intermediate
species trout sea-trout
season april may june july august september
origin region="England, 1860s"
description "A red goose tail, a flat silver body ribbed with fine oval silver, a black hen throat and a wing of peacock sword fibres with a thin red slip along each side."
history """
    A fly of the 1860s or earlier, named after Alexandra, Princess of Wales from 1863. Courtney
    Williams wrote in 1932 that it was first called the Lady of the Lake, but gave no source.
    The earliest dated claim is a letter from "Old Fly", who said he first made and used it in
    May 1863. W. G. Turle is usually credited with the name. The claims were argued over by
    1880: Marston wrote that Turle believed he had invented it, which he thought quite likely,
    and that Dr Brunton said he had, which he thought quite possible. Hobbs and Kelson made
    claims too. Its silver and peacock made it a well-known lake and sea-trout fly. Courtney
    Williams wrote that it had once been barred from some waters for being too deadly, but gave
    no source for that either. The dressing tied in Britain today is the one he gave in 1949:
    red tail and cheeks, once of ibis and now of goose, a silver body, black hackle and a sword
    wing. Ray Bergman's American dressing has a peacock sword tail and a red or claret hackle.
    """
fishing """
    A lake and loch fly, usually fished on the point of a team, often slowly on a sinking line,
    when trout are chasing fry; on rivers it is also a sea-trout fly. The silver and peacock
    flash like a small fish, perhaps a stickleback.
    """
sources {
    "Wikipedia: Alexandra (wet fly)" url="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alexandra_(wet_fly)"
    "Ahrex Hooks: Old wets (Lars Chr. Bentsen, flies by Håkan Karsnäser)" url="https://ahrexhooks.com/2022/02/18/old-wets/"
    "Flies Step by Step: Alexandra (Krystian Niemy)" url="https://www.flies-stepbystep.com/en/flies/wet-flies/alexandra-2/"
    "Puckerbrush Flies: The Alexandra" url="https://puckerbrushflies.com/2013/11/15/the-alexandra/"
    "Current Seams: The Alexandra winged wet (Steve Culton)" url="https://currentseams.com/2013/09/25/the-alexandra-winged-wet/"
    "Rackelhanen: Alexandra (Anders Möllervärn)" url="http://www.rackelhane.se/swe/1460.htm"
    "The Fishing Museum: The Alexandra (Andrew Herd)" url="https://fishingmuseum.org.uk.medlarpress.com/alexandra.html"
    "Caurnie Angling Club: The Alexandra" url="https://www.caurnieangling.club/the-alexandra"
}
// Photos for checking proportions while authoring (pnpm refcheck); never published.
references {
    "https://ahrexhooks.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/AlexPic12-1024x687.jpg" page="https://ahrexhooks.com/2022/02/18/old-wets/" author="Håkan Karsnäser" license="All rights reserved; reference only" flip=#true note="Side view in the vise; the British standard"
    "https://www.flies-stepbystep.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/01/DSCF7455-800x423.jpg" page="https://www.flies-stepbystep.com/en/flies/wet-flies/alexandra-2/" author="Krystian Niemy" license="All rights reserved; reference only" note="Side view, on a longer hook"
}

hook wet-standard size=10
thread black size="8/0"

materials {
    tail duck-quill color=red label="Red goose slip"
    rib oval-tinsel color=silver label="Fine oval silver tinsel"
    body flat-tinsel color=silver
    throat hackle-fibres color=black count=30 label="Black hen hackle fibres"
    wing peacock-sword count=60 color="#3d6e34" label="Peacock sword fibres"
    cheeks duck-quill color=red label="Red goose slips"
}

steps {
    attach-thread at=0.9
    wrap-thread to=0.0
    // Read from the photos: 1.1 to 1.7 gapes past the bend.
    tie-in tail length=1.4 angle=8 width=0.2 note="A narrow slip, on top at the bend."
    tie-in rib
    tie-in body
    wrap-thread to=0.86
    wind body to=0.86 turns=14 note="Down and back again for a smooth double layer."
    tie-off body
    wind rib to=0.86 turns=5
    tie-off rib
    tie-in throat length=1.0 angle=35 side=under note="Reaching about to the hook point."
    // Read from the photos: the tips half a gape to a gape past the bend.
    tie-in wing length=2.6 angle=14 spread=30 note="Equal amounts from a left and a right sword, on top with a pinch and loop."
    tie-in cheeks length=1.9 angle=12 spread=14 width=0.15 note="A thin red slip along each side of the wing."
    whip-finish varnish=#true note="A small black head, varnished."
}
